**assistant**:
> This is likely a `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` issue during `s2`'s vendored Abseil build, not an `sf` issue per se.
> 
> ## What’s going wrong
> 
> `s2` builds its own copy of Abseil, then tries to find it with `pkg-config`. Your manual `configure.vars` is probably interfering with the package’s own path handling.
> 
> The key clue is this:
> 
> ```text
> ** Using PKG_CONFIG_PATH=.::/tmp/.../s2/tools/pkgconfig
> Package absl_base was not found in the pkg-config search path.
> ...
> Package 'absl_base', required by 'absl_s2', not found
> ```
> 
> That suggests the build completed, but the expected `.pc` files either:
> 
> 1. were not installed where `s2` expects, or
> 2. `pkg-config` is not searching the right place because of the custom path value.
> 
> Also, `PKG_CONFIG_PATH=.::...` is suspicious. The `::` creates an empty path element.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## First thing to try: don’t manually install `s2`
> 
> Remove this:
> 
> ```r
> install.packages(
>   "s2", 
>   type = "source",
>   configure.vars = "PKG_CONFIG_PATH=.::./tools/pkgconfig:./tools/build/abseil-cpp/lib/pkgconfig:./tools/build/abseil-cpp/lib64/pkgconfig"
> )
> ```
> 
> and just do:
> 
> ```r
> install.packages("sf", dependencies = TRUE)
> library(sf)
> ```
> 
> Why: `s2`’s build scripts are designed to manage their own vendored Abseil configuration. Overriding `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` with relative paths like `./tools/...` in the package build environment can break that logic.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Better Docker setup
> 
> You likely also want a few more system packages that are commonly needed for compiling R spatial packages:
> 
> ```dockerfile
> RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
>     build-essential \
>     cmake \
>     g++ \
>     gcc \
>     gfortran \
>     make \
>     pkg-config \
>     libgdal-dev \
>     libgeos-dev \
>     libproj-dev \
>     libudunits2-dev \
>     libssl-dev \
>     sqlite3 \
>     libsqlite3-dev \
>     && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
> ```
> 
> A few notes:
> 
> - I would **avoid pinning exact Ubuntu package versions** unless CodeOcean requires it. Those pins often make builds fragile.
> - `build-essential` is important.
> - `libsqlite3-dev` is often helpful for GDAL-related builds.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## If `sf` still tries to pull `s2` and fails
> 
> Depending on the `sf` version, you may be able to install `sf` without `s2` support or use an older version. But before going there, try forcing a clean install of `s2` without custom `configure.vars`.
> 
> In R:
> 
> ```r
> Sys.unsetenv("PKG_CONFIG_PATH")
> install.packages("s2", type = "source")
> install.packages("sf", type = "source")
> library(sf)
> ```
> 
> This is important: your explicit `configure.vars` is the most likely culprit.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## If you want to avoid `s2` entirely
> 
> For older workflows, you can install an older `sf` version from before mandatory `s2` integration was common. For example with `remotes`:
> 
> ```r
> install.packages("remotes")
> remotes::install_version("sf", version = "0.9-8")
> ```
> 
> But I’d treat this as a fallback, not the first fix.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Most likely working solution
> 
> ### Dockerfile
> 
> ```dockerfile
> RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
>     build-essential \
>     cmake \
>     pkg-config \
>     libgdal-dev \
>     libgeos-dev \
>     libproj-dev \
>     libudunits2-dev \
>     libssl-dev \
>     libsqlite3-dev \
>     && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
> ```
> 
> ### R script
> 
> ```r
> Sys.unsetenv("PKG_CONFIG_PATH")
> install.packages("sf", type = "source", dependencies = TRUE)
> library(sf)
> sf::sf_extSoftVersion()
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## If that still fails
> 
> Then I’d inspect whether `s2` is generating the Abseil pkg-config files at all. A useful debugging step is:
> 
> ```r
> install.packages("s2", type = "source", verbose = TRUE)
> ```
> 
> and possibly in Docker:
> 
> ```bash
> find /tmp -name 'absl_base.pc' 2>/dev/null
> find /tmp -path '*s2*pkgconfig*' 2>/dev/null
> ```
> 
> If `absl_base.pc` is never created, then this may be a version-specific `s2`/toolchain issue in that CodeOcean base image.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## My best guess
> 
> The fastest fix is:
> 
> 1. **remove the manual `install.packages("s2", configure.vars=...)`**
> 2. **unset `PKG_CONFIG_PATH`**
> 3. **install `sf` directly**
> 4. **ensure `build-essential` and friends are installed**
> 
> If you want, I can help you rewrite the full Dockerfile and `main.R` for CodeOcean specifically.
